/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The Arduino Nano is a compact, breadboard-friendly microcontroller board developed by Arduino.cc, based on the ATmega328P microcontroller \(or ATmega168 in older versions\). Released in 2008, it measures just 45 x 18 mm and weighs around 7 grams, making it ideal for projects where space is a constraint. Despite its small size, it offers functionality similar to the larger Arduino Uno, featuring 14 digital input/output pins \(6 of which support PWM\), 8 analog inputs, a 16 MHz crystal oscillator, a mini-USB port for programming and power, and an ICSP header for in-circuit programming. It operates at 5V, with an input voltage range of 7-12V \(6-20V possible but not recommended beyond 12V\), and each pin can handle a maximum current of 40mA.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
